    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The hamlet grew around a grist mill built by David Spoor and a sawmill by Samuel A. in the late 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/meridale_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Meridale’s name traces back to the Town of Meredith, organized in 1800 from parts of Franklin and Kortright, named after Philadelphia financier Samuel Meredith.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/meridale_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1888 Francis W. founded Meridale Farms, bringing national attention to the hamlet’s dairy industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/meridale_new_york"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community sits at 1,778 feet on rolling Catskill hills, with the West Branch Delaware River about six miles west, shaping its rural character.",
